## The rule in one paragraph

At the closing auction the account buys NVDA and holds it overnight. At the next opening auction it sells NVDA and buys AAPL. At the closing auction it sells AAPL and buys NVDA again. The core never holds both names at once. Three smaller legs sit beside it: XOM, 20% of NAV while XOM is above its 200-day average (checked once a month); USO, 20% of NAV for 21 trading days after the oil-volatility index OVX closes back below 50; and FTNT, an extra 50% of NAV bought at the open and sold at the close on days it opens at least 2% below the prior close. Nothing is financed overnight, and no order depends on judgment during the session.

{{fig:sizing}}

## Rules

| Leg | Trigger, fixed before the order | Order | Size |
|---|---|---|---|
| Night · NVDA | Every trading day | Buy at the closing auction, sell at the next opening auction | Core |
| Day · AAPL | Every trading day | Buy at the opening auction, sell at the closing auction | Core |
| FTNT overlay | Pre-market indication at 09:28 ET at or below 98% of the prior close; not an earnings day or the day after; prior VIX close below 30 | Buy at the open, sell at the close | 50% of NAV, intraday only |
| XOM | First trading day of the month: XOM closes above its 200-day average | Hold until the next monthly check | 20% of NAV, or nothing |
| USO | OVX closes below 50 after closing at or above 50 the day before | Buy at that close, sell at the close 21 trading days later; re-arms only after OVX is back above 50 | 20% of NAV |
| Core | 1 − XOM weight − USO weight | | 100%, 80% or 60% of NAV |

The backtest charges 1.2 basis points per leg for a round trip, the fee level of auction orders. Slippage beyond that comes straight out of the result. In the registered study (data to Sep 21, 2026), raising the cost to 3 bp per leg cut the ten-year return from 85.0% to 71.5% a year, and 8 bp cut it to 38.8%. That is why only auction orders are allowed.

## Why the day is split this way

Over the registered study's ten years NVDA earned +51.4% a year overnight and +8.6% a year during the session. AAPL earned +1.4% overnight and +27.6% during the session. The rotation holds each stock for the half of the day in which it has earned its return. The mirror image, AAPL overnight and NVDA by day, made +3.6% a year. The pattern holds in both halves of the decade.

The FTNT overlay fired on 77 days in ten years, about eight a year. On those days FTNT rose from open to close 71% of the time, by an average of 122 basis points (t = 4.1), and the worst day was −4.1%. Without the earnings filter the hit rate drops to 64%: a gap-down on the day after earnings behaves like any other day.

XOM and USO are energy exposure with rules attached. The XOM gate helped more when checked monthly than daily or not at all. The USO leg is a probe. Its events are positive on average but not significant, so it runs under its own kill switch.

