## What makes it fragile

- **Hindsight.** NVDA was the strongest large-cap stock of the decade, and the night leg was chosen knowing that. With QQQ in its place the night leg returns about +37% a year. Kill switch F-1 exists for this reason.
- **One stock overnight.** Up to 100% of NAV sits in NVDA every night. In 2022 the night leg alone lost 37.8%. The worst month and the worst day both fell in March 2020, mostly through the night leg.
- **Costs.** Every basis point of slippage per leg costs several points of annual return; see the cost test above.
- **Intraday exposure.** On FTNT trigger days the day book reaches 150% of NAV through intraday buying power, and it is flat by the close.
- **The oil probe can misfire.** OVX fell through 50 on Mar 2, 2020, just before the oil crash. USO lost 57.6% over the next 21 days, about −11.5% of NAV at a 20% weight.

## Kill switches and current readings

The kill switches are armed from the day the rule takes effect, and each one acts mechanically.

| # | Condition | Action | Reading at the Oct 26 close |
|---|---|---|---|
| F-1 | Rolling 252 days: NVDA night leg at or below the QQQ night leg | Night leg switches from NVDA to QQQ | NVDA +60.3% vs QQQ +12.6%: clear |
| F-2 | Drawdown since Oct 26 reaches −40% | Stop R1 and return to the previous rule set | First day |
| F-3 | Rolling 252 days: AAPL day leg at or below zero | Day leg goes to cash | +27.1%: clear |
| F-4 | First eight live USO events sum to zero or less, or one event loses 25% | Close the USO leg | One event on record (Oct 5) |
| F-5 | First 20 days: median auction slippage above 5 bp | Review execution | Collecting |
| F-6 | Last 20 FTNT triggers: under 50% up or a net loss; or one trigger loses 8% | Close the FTNT overlay | 65% up, +17.4% (backtest definition): clear |

Gate status at the Oct 26 close:
- **XOM:** 184.54 against a 200-day average of 152.20, gate open.
- **USO:** the Oct 5 event runs to its 21st trading day on Nov 3.
- **Core:** 60% of NAV.
- **FTNT overlay:** VIX at 18.4, so the overlay is live. FTNT reports on Oct 28, so Oct 28 and Oct 29 are excluded.

## Amendments in the queue

These amendments are registered and cooling. Until their dates R1 runs as described above.

**v1.2 night gate (rule change #20, cooling to Nov 16).** If QQQ closed below its 200-day average the day before, the account skips the NVDA purchase at the close and holds cash overnight. The day leg is unchanged.
- **Nights skipped:** 413 over ten years. On those nights NVDA averaged −13.3 bp with 47% up, against +24.4 bp and 60% up on the others.
- **Backtest:** +93.4% a year, Sharpe 2.36, maximum drawdown −33.5%.
- **2022:** goes from −18.5% to +23.2%.
- **Years that get worse:** 2019, 2020, 2023 and 2026. The gate is slow when a recovery starts below the average.

**S-1 satellite (rule change #19, cooling to Nov 15).** Buy NVDA with up to 10% of NAV, without new margin, when it closes 15% below its one-year high while QQQ is above its 200-day average. Exit after 21 trading days, when QQQ closes below its average, or when NVDA reaches −20% from the high.
- **History since 2014:** 15 events, 80% up after 21 days, average +8.4%.
- **Against any bull-market day:** 68% up and +5.6%. The difference is not significant (p ≈ 0.24).

## What R1 does not do

R1 does not use SQQQ or any other short of the index. It does not time the night or day leg on volatility. It does not hold FTNT every day or finance the energy legs. It does not trade before the open or after the close, and it uses no limit orders and no judgment during the session. Each of these was backtested and rejected.
